ion, I can use that. I'll get all rows in the table and skip the header/filter rows to find data rows. Then, extract the search term from the first cell of each row. I must return the top 2 search terms as a string, e.g., 'term1, term2'. If less than 2 are found, return what's available. I should not overcomplicate; just extract and return the data. No knowledge base functions are provided, so I won't use any. I'll write the code accordingly.",
  "python_code": "async def act(page):\n    # Locate the table in the main section\n    table = page.get_by_role('main').get_by_role('table').first\n    \n    # Get all rows in the table\n    rows = await table.get_by_role('row').all()\n    \n    # Filter out header/filter rows to find data rows containing search terms\n    # Based on the accessibility tree, the first row is for 'All Store Views', which is likely a filter row\n    # Data rows should come after that\n    search_terms = []\n    for row in rows:\n        # Check if this row contains cells with search term data\n        cells = await row.get_by_role('cell').all()\n        if len(cells) > 0:\n            # Get the text of the first cell, which should be the search term\n            cell_text = await cells[0].inner_text()\n            # Skip if it's 'All Store Views' or other non-data text\n            if cell_text.strip() and cell_text != 'All Store Views':\n                search_terms.append(cell_text.strip())\n                if len(search_terms) >= 2:\n                    break\n    \n    # Return the top 2 search terms as a comma-separated string\n    if search_terms:\n        return ', '.join(search_terms[:2])\n    else:\n        return 'No search terms found'",
